  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Rod\u00f3n exited after four innings, but the lineup didn't wait for a second chance. The offense broke it open early and held on.",
    "what_this_means": "They led from the second inning onward, converting three of ten runners left in scoring position into actual runs. Jazz Chisholm Jr. supplied the power with two home runs and two RBIs, but this was not a one-man game. Ben Rice drove in two more. The Yankees pieced together 11 hits and turned them into eight runs against Toronto, the kind of balanced offensive day that doesn't require a single player to carry the entire load. Blackburn and Yarbrough bridged into the late innings cleanly. Two scoreless appearances that let the lead hold. The bullpen did not collapse. The bats did not go quiet after establishing control. This is what execution looks like when it's not complicated: get ahead early, keep adding, finish it.",
    "what_to_watch": "The gap between 6-4 in the last ten games and competing in a division three games back is discipline at the line and relief arms who don't cough it up. They have both today. Tomorrow brings Ethan Pecko, whose 4.91 ERA reads friendly until you see how Houston's offense has been built to take advantage of command issues. They don't swing at much, and when they do, it carries. Will Warren gets his turn.",
